"""Conformance skeleton for a plane package.

Every plane package inherits the same suite. The reference no-op provider's
tests are the worked version of this file — copy from
packages/cruxible-provider-noop/tests/ and replace the interface, the payloads,
and the fixtures.

The obligations, in the order the contract states them:

1.  bind refuses without an accepted artifact (unaccepted_provider);
2.  bind refuses when the package manifest diverges from the accepted artifact
    (manifest_divergence), and when it carries an unknown field
    (unknown_manifest_field);
3.  bind refuses an interface the manifest does not declare
    (undeclared_interface) and a digest the registry does not hold
    (interface_digest_mismatch);
4.  bind refuses an unsupported protocol major (unsupported_protocol) and an
    unsupported backend kind (unsupported_backend);
5.  registration refuses a claimed bucket with no conformance fixture
    (bucket_fixture_missing);
6.  bind refuses a lock that resolves away from the accepted pin (lock_mismatch);
7.  invoke refuses an input classifying into an unclaimed bucket
    (unclaimed_bucket) before any process starts;
8.  a budget breach is a refusal, not a provider error
    (budget_wall_clock, budget_output_size);
9.  a contacted endpoint outside the declaration refuses (undeclared_egress);
10. credential material appears in no argv, no environment block, no cache file,
    no digest preimage, and no trace — including when the provider tries;
11. success, typed refusal, and error paths all work on BOTH backend kinds;
12. one per-bucket fixture per claimed bucket, each of which actually passes;
13. every extra the manifest requires exists in the distribution, and an
    artifact that pins some other implementation's environment refuses
    (lock_mismatch, naming the environment pin key) rather than binding it;
14. a run that needs an engine the environment does not carry refuses
    (environment_divergence) rather than raising ImportError across the process
    boundary.

No test in the default lane may require a network, a container engine, or a
heavy engine. Tests that drive a real engine are marked `engine`, are excluded
from the default run, and skip with a reason when the engine is absent -- so the
lane still COLLECTS on a machine that has none, which is what keeps it from
rotting unnoticed.
"""
